(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-1b-1c-quiz"]={"title":"Class IB & IC Antiarrhythmics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug lists agranulocytosis among its toxicities?","choices":["Propafenone","Flecainide","Lidocaine","Disopyramide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone's toxicity list includes agranulocytosis, anemia and thrombocytopenia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Why is lidocaine given intravenously?","choices":["Extensive first-pass metabolism","A half-life of about 40 days","Elimination unchanged by the kidneys","A duration of action of 3 to 4 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism, so it is given intravenously rather than by mouth. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which arrhythmia can flecainide cause that resists treatment?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Sinus bradycardia","Ventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list includes ventricular tachycardia resistant to treatment, a form of the proarrhythmia every antiarrhythmic can cause. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which respiratory effect of flecainide matters in a patient with asthma?","choices":["Pneumonitis","Respiratory paralysis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Bronchospasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is on flecainide's toxicity list (and propafenone's), a concern in asthma. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which cardiac effect is on lidocaine's toxicity list?","choices":["Slower atrioventricular conduction","Faster atrioventricular conduction","Torsades de pointes","Decreased contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine's toxicity list includes increased atrioventricular conduction, along with hypersensitivity and its central nervous system effects. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug is a class IC antiarrhythmic?","choices":["Flecainide","Lidocaine","Quinidine","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide is class IC (propafenone is the other); it blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is propafenone a concern in a patient with heart failure?","choices":["It raises digoxin levels","It can worsen heart failure","It increases atrioventricular conduction","It causes a lupus-like syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone can worsen congestive heart failure, an effect of its beta-blocking activity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which drug is the class IB antiarrhythmic?","choices":["Flecainide","Procainamide","Propafenone","Lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is the class IB sodium channel blocker; it works mainly on sodium channels in ischemic tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which setting is a listed indication for lidocaine?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Torsades de pointes","Acute myocardial infarction","Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is listed for ventricular tachycardia and premature beats, ventricular fibrillation and acute myocardial infarction, where ischemic tissue is its target. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where do class IB drugs have their greatest effect?","choices":["Normal ventricular tissue","The atria","Ischemic tissue","The sinoatrial node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class IB drugs have a higher affinity in ischemic tissue and little effect in normal tissue; lidocaine is most effective on sodium channels in ischemic tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which arrhythmia is a listed indication for flecainide?","choices":["Torsades de pointes","Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia","Ventricular arrhythmia after infarction","Rate slowing in atrial fibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide is listed for life-threatening sustained ventricular arrhythmias and supraventricular arrhythmias, in patients without significant structural heart damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Lidocaine is ineffective against which type of arrhythmia?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Premature ventricular beats","Atrial arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine works on sodium channels in ischemic tissue, has less effect on normal tissue, and is not effective against atrial arrhythmias. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which pharmacokinetic feature is listed for flecainide?","choices":["Extensive first-pass metabolism","A long half-life of 14 hours","A half-life of about 10 seconds","Elimination unchanged by the kidneys"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide has a long half-life (14 hours) and can be given by mouth or intravenously. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which feature separates class IC from class IB?","choices":["IC works mainly in ischemic tissue","IC shortens phase 3","IC affects the normal heart","IC blocks L-type calcium channels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class IC slows phase 0 and conduction, prolongs the refractory period and affects the normal heart; class IB has little effect on normal tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
